SEND: {"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 13, "result": {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "--- FILE: /data/data/com.termux/files/home/Projects/agy-nodeos/physics_optimization_blueprint.md (57 lines) ---\n   1 | # Physics Engine Optimization Blueprint\n   2 | \n   3 | ## 1. O(N log N) QuadTree Approximation\n   4 | To eliminate the O(N^2) nested loop that calculates Coulomb Repulsion, we leverage the existing `QuadTree` spatial index. By querying only for local nodes within a 150px interaction radius, we drastically reduce the distance calculations per tick.\n   5 | \n   6 | **File:** `nodes_engine.py`\n   7 | **Method:** `NativeNodesEngine.simulate_physics(self, ticks=50)`\n   8 | \n   9 | Replaced the naive O(N^2) `for i in range(len(nodes)): for j in range(i+1, len(nodes)):` loop with the following optimized snippet:\n  10 | \n  11 | ```python\n  12 |             # ... Inside the `for tick in range(ticks):` loop ...\n  13 |             \n  14 |             # Rebuild QuadTree for rapid O(N log N) spatial queries\n  15 |             self.rebuild_qtree()\n  16 |             \n  17 |             for node in self.all_nodes:\n  18 |                 # 1. Central Gravity\n  19 |                 node.fx = (center_x - node.x) * k_gravity\n  20 |                 node.fy = (center_y - node.y) * k_gravity\n  21 |                 \n  22 |                 # 2. Optimized Local Repulsion: Query QuadTree for nearby nodes\n  23 |                 range_rect = Rectangle(node.x, node.y, 150, 150)\n  24 |                 nearby_nodes = self.qtree.query(range_rect)\n  25 |                 \n  26 |                 for other in nearby_nodes:\n  27 |                     if other == node:\n  28 |                         continue\n  29 |                     dx, dy = node.x - other.x, node.y - other.y\n  30 |                     dist_sq = dx**2 + dy**2\n  31 |                     if dist_sq > 0:\n  32 |                         force = k_repulse / dist_sq\n  33 |                         dist = math.sqrt(dist_sq)\n  34 |                         node.fx += force * (dx / dist)\n  35 |                         node.fy += force * (dy / dist)\n  36 | ```\n  37 | \n  38 | ## 2. Decoupling the Physics Simulation from the Event Loop\n  39 | To prevent `cold_start_ingestion()` from blocking the main `daemon.py` asyncio event loop, we must offload the massive CPU-bound physics calculations to a background daemon thread.\n  40 | \n  41 | **File:** `nodes_engine.py`\n  42 | **Method:** `NativeNodesEngine.resolve_edges(self)`\n  43 | \n  44 | Located the end of the `resolve_edges` method where `self.simulate_physics()` is called synchronously. Replaced it with the following code to spawn it in a `threading.Thread`:\n  45 | \n  46 | ```python\n  47 |         print(f\"[Physics Engine] Bonded {edge_count} structural connections. Simulating graph clustering...\")\n  48 |         \n  49 |         if edge_count > 0:\n  50 |             import threading\n  51 |             # Run physics simulation in the background so it doesn't block the Daemon event loop\n  52 |             threading.Thread(target=self.simulate_physics, args=(100,), daemon=True).start()\n  53 | ```\n  54 | \n  55 | ## Summary of Impact\n  56 | - **CPU Time:** Reduced from O(N^2) calculations per tick (1.25 billion on 50k nodes) to O(N log N), practically eliminating the 96% CPU freeze.\n  57 | - **Event Loop Unblocked:** By wrapping the simulation call in `threading.Thread(daemon=True).start()`, `cold_start_ingestion()` instantly returns execution context to the `AGYNodeOSEventHandler`, allowing the zero-dependency Raw Watchdog to begin observing file changes immediately while the physics simulate silently in the background.\n"}]}}
